    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933939/assets/c9c426e75153bd893e3dbef39bd8a837991d8c3ec6a48b41aeb27df6c4250660.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=2x-1$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$ What is the equation of the line ${l}_{2}?$",
    "worked_solution": "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $2,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and a $y$-intercept of $6.$ Therefore, it can be written in slope-intercept form as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+6.\n$$",
